<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirza Hasan Shirazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirza Hasan Shirazi was an Iranian Shi'i jurist, the leading religious authority of his time, who taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. In December 1891 a ruling attributed to him declared the use of tobacco forbidden while a British company held the Iranian tobacco monopoly, and the resulting nationwide boycott, the Tobacco Protest, forced Naser al-Din Shah to cancel the concession in 1892.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirza-hasan-shirazi.jpg" alt="Mirza Hasan Shirazi" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirza_Sayyid_Mohammed_Hassan_Al-Husseini_Al-Shirazi_praying.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> on 25 April 1815 into the Shirazi family, Shirazi lost his father, Mirza Mahmud Shirazi, while young and was raised by his maternal uncle, Sayyid Husayn Musawi. He began religious study at four, studied jurisprudence in the seminary of Shiraz and from 1832 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, where he was granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a> in 1835. In 1845 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and studied under senior scholars including Sheikh Muhammad-Hassan Najafi, who gave him a second certificate of ijtihad. On the death of Murtadha Ansari in 1864 he was named the scholar best qualified to succeed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, Shirazi came to be regarded as the leading marja' of the Shia world. Two scholars brought the tobacco concession to his notice, and on 26 July 1891 he sent a telegram to Naser al-Din Shah protesting the monopoly. The shah sent his ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to argue that the deal served the nation. After a second appeal went unanswered, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> began to circulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> declaring the use of tobacco equal to war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Hidden Imam</a>, Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. Doubts about whether it was genuine faded when Shirazi did not disown it.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> his standing drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> mourning ceremonies, and he gave money to help them finish a seminary of their own. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> answered by founding more Sunni madrasas in the town. In 1893 sectarian violence there killed his son Muhammad and a nephew. Shirazi restrained Shias from the south who wanted to come and fight, and he turned away British and Russian offers of support, calling the matter a dispute among brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shirazi died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> on 20 February 1895 and was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-ali-shrine">Imam Ali Shrine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. His duties left him little time to write, and he produced only a few works on the writings of Ansari. His students included Muhammad-Kadhim Khurasani, Muhammad-Kadhim Tabatabaei, Muhammad-Husayn Naini and Fadhlallah Nuri. Historians have described his part in the tobacco boycott as a striking demonstration of the power of the marja'-i taqlid, and the protest has been cited among the causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirza al-Shirazi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protest spread first among the bazaari and then the ulama, and in December 1891 the marja'-e taqlid <b>Mirza Hasan Shirazi</b> declared the use of tobacco tantamount to war against the Hidden Imam.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.61,52.53&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Shiraz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mirza Hasan Shirazi"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4446 5015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4446 5015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.61,52.53&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
